+++ b/DIFFERENCES.md
@@ -37,3 +37,19 @@ The loadgen this repository used before Hyperfoil parsed its 1.87 MB contract on
 the measured window (about 7.5 ms each), so its 1,024- and 4,096-connection steps started late. That also
 affected the article's numbers at those connection counts; DHH's runs (16 connections, the route
 contract) were barely affected. This repository no longer uses it.
+
+## The Java port
+
+[once-campfire-java](https://github.com/kgonia/once-campfire-java) is measured as a fourth app (`--apps
+...,java`), checked out next to this repository (`JAVA_PORT` overrides). It is a whole Campfire on Quarkus
+REST, built as a drop-in for the Rails app rather than for this page. The rule it is held to is **the same
+content**, not the same bytes: what the page says and how it is structured must match Rust's, while how
+it is spelled and compressed is the app's own design.
+
+| | Rust, Kotlin, Quarkus | Java port | Why |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| The smoke test after every start | each decoded body byte-identical to Rust's page | each decoded body identical to Rust's page **as markup**: the Java port's `tools/parity/markup.py`, one token per tag or text run, attributes decoded and sorted, whitespace collapsed (`same_page` in `bench.sh`) | jte and Askama spell the same elements differently. On the seed both pages have 7,497 tokens, all equal (7,557 on the desktop-browser branch, all equal). The bytes differ by 0.3% (417,243 against 416,116). The check rejects a one-word change. |
+| The gzipped page on the wire | Rust 24,229 bytes; Kotlin and Quarkus 24,227 | 20,678 bytes, 15% fewer | All use deflate at level 6. Rust and the Kotlin modules compress each message fragment as a piece of its own and splice the pieces together; the Java port splices only the fragments every page shares (the importmap and stylesheet tags) and compresses the rest of the page as one stream, which compresses better. Fewer bytes per cached page is part of its design: count it when reading all-out requests per second, where writing the page is much of the work. |
+| Response headers | Rust's | Rust's less `X-Request-Id`, `X-Runtime` and `X-Cache`; `Vary: Accept-Encoding` alone; cookies with `Max-Age`; `Cache-Control` directives in another order | Deliberate in the Java port (its README, "Known differences from the Rust port"). Not compared by the smoke test. A cached page's headers are 1,544 bytes against the others' 1,657. |
+| JVM | Kotlin: Temurin 25; Quarkus: Temurin 27, plus the UBI image's and Netty's options | Temurin 25 (`JDK_JAVA`), the options every JVM app gets (`JVM_COMMON`: ParallelGC, compact headers, `-Xmx512m`) and nothing more | Its own image runs ZGC with a 1 GB soft maximum; here it gets the shared baseline. Its README measures `-Xmx256m` at 6% slower on the room page. |
+| SQLite | Kotlin and Quarkus: FFM; Rust: rusqlite | sqlite-jdbc through Agroal, one writer and up to 16 readers, statements cached per connection | As published. It creates Rust's two indexes on `messages` at boot, as Rust and Quarkus do. |
